B.aZD=new A.hN("The Greeting Is a Qur\u2019anic Sentence","\u0627\u0644\u0633\u064e\u0644\u0627\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0643\u064f\u0645\u0652","As-salamu alaykum \u2014 peace be upon you \u2014 is a Qur\u2019anic formula (6:54: peace be upon you for what you patiently endured). The reply, wa alaykum as-salam, mirrors it. Every time you greet a Muslim, you are speaking Qur\u2019anic Arabic.","Qur\u2019an 6:54",null,null)
B.aZv=new A.hN("Family Words You Already Own","\u0623\u064e\u0628\u064c / \u0623\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064c / \u0627\u0628\u0652\u0646\u064c / \u0628\u0646\u062a\u064c","Ab (father), umm (mother), ibnu/bint (son/daughter) \u2014 the same roots that carry the Qur\u2019an\u2019s family passages (12:100; 71:28). Learn these four and you can read entire family verses without translation.","Qur\u2019an 12:100; 71:28",null,null)
B.aZA=new A.hN("The Definite Article: Al-","\u0627\u0644\u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u2014 \u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c",u.N,"Grammar note",null,null)
B.aZu=new A.hN("Allah Is the Name, Rabb Is the Word","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","Allah is the proper Name; rabb is the noun (Lord, Nurturer). You cannot change rabb into ar-rabb the way you can add al- to kitab \u2014 some words resist the article. Qur\u2019anic Arabic teaches its own rules.","Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","See it in Al-Fatiha")
B.asy=s([B.aZD,B.aZv,B.aZA,B.aZu],t.n1)
B.aZM=new A.vH("First Words, Real Words",B.asy)
B.aZB=new A.hN("Al-Fatiha, Word by Word","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e",u.n,"Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","Recite it in the reader")
B.aZz=new A.hN("The Formula You Say 17 Times a Day","\u0625\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0643\u064e \u0646\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f","Iyyaka na\u2019budu \u2014 You alone we worship. Iyyaka carries the kasra twice (to You alone), and na\u2019budu is the present-tense verb. Salah is a daily Arabic lesson the body never forgets.","Qur\u2019an 1:5",null,null)
B.aZI=new A.hN("Slow Is the Standard","\u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062a\u0651\u0650\u0644\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u064f\u0631\u0652\u0622\u0646\u064e \u062a\u064e\u0631\u0652\u062a\u0650\u064a\u0644\u0627\u064b","And recite the Qur\u2019an with measured recitation (73:4). Slow enough to hear each letter. Arabic learned slowly stays; Arabic rushed evaporates.","Qur\u2019an 73:4",null,null)
B.azJ=s([B.aZB,B.aZz,B.aZI],t.n1)
B.aZJ=new A.vH("Reading Whole Verses",B.azJ)
B.aZC=new A.hN("The Simplest Sentence: Ism and Fi'l","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Arabic needs only two words for a full sentence: a name (ism) and an action (fi'l). Allah rabbuna \u2014 Allah is our Lord. The ism 'Allah' and the fi'l 'rabbuna' carry the whole meaning. Every sentence you will ever read is built from these two.","Grammar note",null,null)
B.aZw=new A.hN("The Verb: Past and Present","\u062e\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0642\u064e \u0648\u064e\u064a\u064e\u062e\u0644\u064f\u0642\u064f","Khalaqa \u2014 He created (past). Yakhlugu \u2014 He creates (present). The past is fixed; the present is alive. Arabic shows time in the verb's shape, not in extra words. Learn khalaqa and you have the template for every past verb in the Qur'an.","Qur'an 16:40 pattern",null,null)
B.aZF=new A.hN("The Feminine Marker: The Round Ta","\u0631\u064e\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064c \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064e\u0629\u064c","The round ta (\u0629) at the end of a word often marks the feminine: rahim (merciful, masculine) and rahimah (merciful, feminine). The basmala uses both: al-Rahman, al-Rahim \u2014 the two most merciful Names, one masculine form, one with the round ta.","Qur'an 1:1",null,null)
B.aZG=new A.hN("The Broken Plural","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c \u2192 \u0643\u064f\u062a\u064f\u0628\u064c","Not every plural adds a sound ending. Kitab (book) becomes kutub (books) \u2014 the vowels inside change shape. This is the broken plural, and it is everywhere in the Qur'an. Learn it as a pattern to recognize, not a rule to fear.","Qur'an 2:2 (al-kutub)",null,null)
B.ahZ=s([B.aZC,B.aZw,B.aZF,B.aZG],t.n1)
B.aZK=new A.vH("Building Sentences",B.ahZ)
B.aZx=new A.hN("I, You, He \u2014 in the Qur'an","\u0623\u064e\u0646\u0627\u0653 \u0623\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f\u0643\u064e","Ana \u2014 I. Anta \u2014 you (masculine). Anti \u2014 you (feminine). Huwa \u2014 he. Hiya \u2014 she. In Al-Fatiha you say ana and ka together: a'budu-ka \u2014 I worship You. The pronoun -ka (you) rides on the end of the verb, invisible until you learn to see it.","Qur'an 1:5",null,null)
B.aZH=new A.hN("The Attached Pronoun: -ka and -ki","\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650\u064a \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0643\u064e \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Rabbi (my Lord), rabbuka (your Lord, masculine), rabbuki (your Lord, feminine), rabbuna (our Lord). One word, four meanings, one small sound added. This is Arabic's economy \u2014 a single attached letter carries a whole relationship.","Qur'an 1:2 (rabbina)",null,null)
B.aZE=new A.hN("The Dual: Two of Something","\u064a\u064e\u0648\u0652\u0645\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u0650","Yawm (day) becomes yawmayni (two days) \u2014 the dual ending -ayni. The Qur'an uses it precisely: the two gardens of the man in Surah 18, the two days of creation in Surah 41. Arabic counts two as its own number, not as 'two ones.'","Qur'an 18:32 (al-jannatani)",null,null)
B.axP=s([B.aZx,B.aZH,B.aZE],t.n1)
B.aZL=new A.vH("The Pronouns You Pray With",B.axP)
B.aZs=new A.hN("The Idaafa: Possession in Two Words","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650","Kitabu Allah \u2014 the Book of Allah. Two nouns side by side, and the relationship is possession. The first word loses its 'the' (kitabu, not al-kitabu); the second keeps it (Allah, already definite). This construction is everywhere \u2014 every 'of' in translation is often an idaafa in Arabic.","Qur'an 2:2 (kitabu Allah)",null,null)
B.aZt=new A.hN("The Diptotes: Names That Refuse","\u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064c \u0644\u0627 \u064a\u064e\u0642\u0628\u064e\u0644\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0652","Proper names like Muhammad, Ahmad, and many broken plurals refuse the 'the' (al-) in certain positions. Muhammadun, not al-Muhammadun. The Qur'an respects the name's dignity \u2014 learn to spot the names that stand without the article.","Qur'an 3:144 (Muhammadun)",null,null)
B.aZy=new A.hN("Putting It Together: One Verse, Fully","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","You now know every word of this verse: the definite article (al-), the idaafa (lillahi), the attached pronoun (implicit in lillahi), the noun of lordship (rabbi), the broken plural (alamin). Read it again \u2014 you are not transliterating anymore. You are reading.","Qur'an 1:2","/quran","Read it in the reader")
B.afe=s([B.aZs,B.aZt,B.aZy],t.n1)
B.aZN=new A.vH("Reading With Possession",B.afe)
B.awt=s([B.aZM,B.aZJ,B.aZK,B.aZL,B.aZN],A.a9("y<vH>"))
